package mop;

import javax.crypto.KeyGenerator;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateStore;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateVerdict;
import br.unb.cic.mop.Property;

/**
 * SecretKeySpec
 *
 * A JavaMOP specification of the correct usage of the
 * javax.crypto.spec.SecretKey.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/blob/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SecretKey.crysl
 */
SecretKeySpec(SecretKey secretKey) {

   // The bytes `getEncoded()` returned, held from the event that observed them to the
   // handler that may write about them. A handler sees no event parameter, and the
   // acceptance point is where the clause says the predicate is established, so the two
   // have to be joined by a field -- the same shape SecureRandomSpec's `next2` uses for
   // the array it marks.
   byte[] stagedKeyMaterial;

   // The whole of this specification: reading a key's encoding. api30 ENSURES
   // `preparedKeyMaterial[keyMaterial] after ge` (SecretKey.cryptsl:25) and states no
   // REQUIRES section at all, so nothing here translates a constraint and nothing here
   // accuses -- this event has no code in `codes.csv` and gains none.
   //
   // What it does is carry a predicate across a call that copies. `getEncoded()` returns a
   // fresh clone each time it is called (measured: two calls on one key return arrays that
   // are not the same object), and the store is keyed on object identity, so no reader of
   // the returned bytes could ever see what was established about the material they were
   // copied from. This event is the only thing in the set that bridges that copy, and that
   // is what makes its read a propagation record rather than an accuser.
   //
   // The read moved out of `condition(...)` -- it was the last one of the set to do so. A
   // condition compiles to `if (!(guard)) return false;` ahead of the body and the
   // transition, so a key whose origin the monitor had not observed left the automaton
   // instead of simply not propagating. Here the transition is taken either way and the
   // verdict decides one thing only: whether there is anything to carry.
   //
   // The verdict is three-valued and this is the one site of the set where the three
   // collapse into two, because the read governs a write instead of a report. SATISFIED
   // stages; NOT_OBSERVED and VIOLATED both stage nothing, and the difference between them
   // has no reader to tell it to.
   //
   // The read stays, rather than the write becoming unconditional as the literal shape of
   // an ENSURES with no REQUIRES would have it, and one measurement decided that. Made
   // unconditional, a key whose material was hard-coded hands its encoding on as
   // randomised: SecretKeySpecSpec still accuses the construction, but the IV built from
   // those bytes stops being accused at all, so the set trades a report about a real defect
   // for a chain it closed one call earlier
   // (data/gh105/evidence/f2-SecretKeySpec.md, configuration D).
   //
   // What the read asks about is `generatedKey` and what it writes is `randomized`, where
   // the rule names `preparedKeyMaterial` on both sides of the copy. That conflation is the
   // seed's, it is ledger clause #32, and it is undone at task 5.10 together with 6.1 --
   // recorded here, not repaired, exactly as task 4.10 recorded it at the other end of the
   // same chain. Renaming the predicate here alone would leave this producer writing what
   // none of the seven migrated readers of `randomized` over a byte[] ask for.
   //
   // api30 also declares `NEGATES generatedKey[this, _] after d`, the second of the
   // oracle's two NEGATES clauses, and this file declares no event for `destroy()`. The
   // omission is deliberate and recorded (INV-INS-137): measured, `destroy()` throws
   // DestroyFailedException on both SecretKey implementations this set can observe -- the
   // SecretKeySpec written here and the one KeyGenerator.generateKey() returns, which is
   // the same class -- so an `after ... returning` advice over it has no execution path, the
   // position SECRETKEYSPEC-CONSTR-01 is already in. Declaring the event would also add a
   // symbol to the automaton, and this specification is one of the thirteen whose ORDER
   // mapping task 7.1 owns.
   event e1 after(SecretKey secretKey) returning(byte[] key):
      call(public byte[] SecretKey.getEncoded()) &&
      target(secretKey) {
        PredicateVerdict generated = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_KEY, secretKey);
        if (generated == PredicateVerdict.SATISFIED) {
            stagedKeyMaterial = key;
        }
   }

   // The automaton and the handler below it are the acceptance point of
   // `preparedKeyMaterial[keyMaterial] after ge`, reached by both of the routes
   // INV-INS-134 admits at once. The clause carries an `after ge`, so it is established at
   // the states `ge` leads to; the `ere` is `e1*`, whose only alias is `match` over its
   // accepting states. Read from the generated monitor, the transition row for `e1` is
   // {0, 1} and the match category is `nextstate == 0`, so the states after `ge` and the
   // accepting states are the same single state, and the handler is both.
   //
   // It runs after every `e1`, which is what the clause needs: each call returns its own
   // copy and each copy has to be marked separately. The staged array is consumed there or
   // discarded, so a call whose key origin was not observed never leaves a marking behind
   // for the next one.
   ere : e1*

   @match {
     if (stagedKeyMaterial != null) {
         PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.RANDOMIZED, stagedKeyMaterial);
         stagedKeyMaterial = null;
     }
   }

}
